{:edb/db-before-t 1958 :edb/db-after-t 1959 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535271 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:58:15.011Z" 13194139535271 true] [17592186124310 1100 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}$." 13194139535271 false] [17592186124310 1100 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}.$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186124310 1145 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^4$. Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{3x^{4} + 9x}{6x^{4} - x^{3}} &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{3x^{4}}{x^{4}} + \\frac{9x}{x^{4}})}{(\\frac{6x^{4}}{x^{4}} - \\frac{x^{3}}{x^{4}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(3 + \\frac{9}{x^{3}})}{(6 - \\frac{1}{x})} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 0}{6 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535271 false] [17592186124310 1145 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^4.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{4}+9x}{6{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{9x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186124312 1100 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}$." 13194139535271 false] [17592186124312 1100 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}.$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186124312 1145 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^2$. Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{4 - 5x - 2x^{3}}{x^{2} + 3} &= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{-2x^{3}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{5x}{x^{2}} + \\frac{4}{x^{2}})}{(\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} + \\frac{3}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{(-2x - \\frac{5}{x} + \\frac{4}{x^{2}})}{(1 + \\frac{3}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}(\\frac{-2x + 0 + 0}{1 + 0}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}(-2x) \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535271 false] [17592186124312 1145 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^2.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4-5x-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-2x-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{-2x+0+0}{1+0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186124313 1100 "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ with $f(x)=\\dfrac{2x^3+1}{x^3-15x}$?" 13194139535271 false] [17592186124313 1100 "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ with $f(x)=\\dfrac{2x^3+1}{x^3-15x}?$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186124313 1145 "The horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{2x^{3} + 1}{x^{3} - 15x} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})}{(\\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{15x}{x^{3}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(2 + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})}{(1 - \\frac{15}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 + 0}{1 - 0} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2$." 13194139535271 false] [17592186124313 1145 "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{3}-15x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{15}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186124326 1100 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^5-10}{x^3-8x^2}$." 13194139535271 false] [17592186124326 1100 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^5-10}{x^3-8x^2}.$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186124326 1145 "The horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{x^{5} - 10}{x^{3} - 8x^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{x^{5}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{10}{x^{3}})}{(\\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{8x^{2}}{x^{3}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(x^{2} - \\frac{10}{x^{3}})}{(1 - \\frac{8}{x})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}(\\frac{x^{2} - 0}{1 - 0}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}x^{2} \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139535271 false] [17592186124326 1145 "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}-10}{{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{8}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{1-0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139535271 true] [17592186124582 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341014 13194139535271 true] [17592186124582 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341040 13194139535271 true] [17592186124582 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341047 13194139535271 true] [17592186124582 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341054 13194139535271 true] [17592186124584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341011 13194139535271 true] [17592186124584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341019 13194139535271 true] [17592186124584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341026 13194139535271 true] [17592186124584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341033 13194139535271 true] [17592186124585 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340997 13194139535271 true] [17592186124585 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341004 13194139535271 true] [17592186124585 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341061 13194139535271 true] [17592186124827 1100 "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\frac{2x + 5}{2x^{3} + 2x + 6}$." 13194139535271 false] [17592186124827 1100 "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6}.$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186125005 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535271 true] [17592186125005 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{x^{4} - 6}{x^{2} - x + 2}$." 13194139535271 false] [17592186125005 1100 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-6}{{x}^{2}-x+2}.$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186125005 1145 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-6}{{x}^{2}-x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-0)}{(1-0+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186275175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341018 13194139535271 true] [17592186340997 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340998 13194139535271 true] [17592186340997 1089 "q-101319" 13194139535271 true] [17592186340997 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535271 true] [17592186340997 1100 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-1}{3x+2}.$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186340997 1113 #uuid "7e63b016-2ed8-5744-85e3-ba7a5b9b598c" 13194139535271 true] [17592186340997 1145 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-1}{3x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-\\frac{1}{x})}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-0)}{(3+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139535271 true] [17592186340998 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340999 13194139535271 true] [17592186340998 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341000 13194139535271 true] [17592186340998 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341001 13194139535271 true] [17592186340998 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341002 13194139535271 true] [17592186340998 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341003 13194139535271 true] [17592186340998 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535271 true] [17592186340998 1041 "selection" 13194139535271 true] [17592186340998 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341000 13194139535271 true] [17592186340998 1110 #uuid "03860e69-abc0-52cb-82c9-f3068c82c0b4" 13194139535271 true] [17592186340999 1034 "y=-\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535271 true] [17592186340999 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186340999 1111 #uuid "57c1e2df-8bcc-5011-9db9-11fe9a6c4695" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341000 1034 "No horizontal asymptote" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341000 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535271 true] [17592186341000 1111 #uuid "5ec15b0d-5160-5486-9468-9ac72d0d9094" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341001 1034 "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341001 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341001 1111 #uuid "0ec79a22-6eef-524b-a114-f45466b6c116" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341002 1034 "y=\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341002 1111 #uuid "9881eff1-19d5-5d12-bf10-b8cf9d59edb6" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341003 1034 "y=\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341003 1111 #uuid "9c15ce9b-efe1-5ee8-8f34-4f3b2207e155" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341004 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341005 13194139535271 true] [17592186341004 1089 "q-106698" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341004 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535271 true] [17592186341004 1100 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}}{6-2x}.$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341004 1113 #uuid "c1e689ad-107f-573d-b38b-7f1ffbb249ba" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341004 1145 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}}{6-2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{5}}{x}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{2x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7{x}^{4}-3x)}{(\\frac{6}{x}-2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7{x}^{4}-3x)}{0-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(7{x}^{4}-3x) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139535271 true] [17592186341005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341006 13194139535271 true] [17592186341005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341007 13194139535271 true] [17592186341005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341008 13194139535271 true] [17592186341005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341009 13194139535271 true] [17592186341005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341010 13194139535271 true] [17592186341005 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535271 true] [17592186341005 1041 "selection" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341005 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341007 13194139535271 true] [17592186341005 1110 #uuid "d33fcbe4-277c-5357-8945-5308d3b4c70e" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341006 1034 "y=-3" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341006 1111 #uuid "825fabcc-dc77-50c1-a74d-517d6520c732" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341007 1034 "No horizontal asymptote" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341007 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535271 true] [17592186341007 1111 #uuid "2d44cd0e-f0c7-5440-bc72-34ea3857082a" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341008 1034 "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341008 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341008 1111 #uuid "d3db3c90-e72d-5b7b-ac9c-d2cac507767b" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341009 1034 "y=2" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341009 1111 #uuid "400914b4-c673-58c8-95b6-7a1a476bb1b5" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341010 1034 "y=\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341010 1111 #uuid "cc483799-152e-5118-9756-a2d30022137e" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341011 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341012 13194139535271 true] [17592186341011 1089 "q-302242" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341011 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535271 true] [17592186341011 1100 "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}}$ is $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ find the value of $c.$\n\n$c=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341011 1113 #uuid "9f9b3b79-6712-5d2b-9420-7d4b4caf5430" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341011 1145 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{18{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(18+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(c-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{18+0}{c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{c} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 36 & =3c \\\\ c & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341012 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341013 13194139535271 true] [17592186341012 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535271 true] [17592186341012 1041 "field-1" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341012 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341013 13194139535271 true] [17592186341012 1110 #uuid "7a793526-c408-5e79-a943-5c9ae00c391c" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341013 1034 "12" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341013 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341013 1111 #uuid "87d01e32-49c1-59bc-b6e4-5333dcefcddc" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341014 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341015 13194139535271 true] [17592186341014 1089 "q-339426" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341014 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535271 true] [17592186341014 1100 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4}=\\frac{2}{5},$  what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341014 1113 #uuid "19f08822-38c8-5644-ae87-60cc80530130" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341014 1145 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{\\,{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(k+\\frac{7}{x}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0-8}{k+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{k} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2k & =-40 \\\\ k & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341016 13194139535271 true] [17592186341015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341017 13194139535271 true] [17592186341015 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535271 true] [17592186341015 1041 "field-1" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341015 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341017 13194139535271 true] [17592186341015 1110 #uuid "fa9fc928-6a48-5a69-891b-6361fb00fd55" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341016 1034 "20" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341016 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341016 1111 #uuid "df03a4ca-7222-5b90-ab10-7896e65d7dd2" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341017 1034 "-20" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341017 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341017 1111 #uuid "fcbd9826-5bdd-5cd8-a2f1-e2ccfd185fab" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341018 1034 "0" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341018 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535271 true] [17592186341018 1111 #uuid "2a276290-ab0d-52b1-8807-4a9062f36d49" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341019 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341020 13194139535271 true] [17592186341019 1089 "q-35829" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341019 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535271 true] [17592186341019 1100 "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8}$ is $y=\\frac{1}{4},$ find the value of $c.$" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341019 1113 #uuid "dfebc30a-ea33-5139-a1fd-71dc23f9282b" 13194139535271 true] [17592186341019 1145 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\fra